  <h1>Disclaimer</h1>
  <p class="lede">Version 2.0 &middot; Last updated: October 21, 2026 &middot; Applies to daoismhub.com and data.daoismhub.com</p>

  <h2>1. Educational and editorial purpose only</h2>
  <p>
    DaoismHub is a reference publication about Chinese contemplative traditions — their
    texts, history, concepts, practices, modern research and recorded practitioner
    experience. Everything published here is provided for general information, study and
    scholarly interest. It is not advice of any kind, it is not tailored to any individual,
    and it is not a substitute for consultation with a qualified professional.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Not medical advice</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing on these sites is intended to diagnose, treat, cure, mitigate or prevent any
    disease or condition. When the site discusses meditation or contemplative-practice
    research, it reports what studies found, in what populations and with what limitations —
    it does not endorse a practice as a treatment. Do not start, stop or change any medical
    or psychological treatment on the basis of anything you read here.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>If you think you may have a medical emergency, contact your local emergency
    services immediately.</strong> Do not use this site to decide whether to seek care.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Not mental-health advice</h2>
  <p>
    Meditation is not a substitute for psychotherapy, psychiatric care or crisis support. If
    you are in distress, if you experience depression, anxiety, dissociation, intrusive
    thoughts or unusual perceptual experiences that concern you, seek help from a qualified
    mental-health professional. Descriptions of contemplative practice on this site are
    historical and ethnographic, not therapeutic guidance.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Not religious, legal or financial advice</h2>
  <p>
    The site describes traditions; it does not teach a religion, grant credentials, or
    confer any authority to teach. Nothing here is legal, tax, financial or investment
    advice. Reading the site creates no teacher–student, practitioner–client, adviser or
    fiduciary relationship of any kind.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Practice safety</h2>
  <p>
    Some practices described on this site are physically or psychologically demanding —
    prolonged sitting, breath regulation, breath retention or hyperventilation, fasting,
    sleep restriction, extreme cold or heat exposure, and intensive retreat formats. These
    are <strong>documented, not prescribed</strong>. They are recorded because the tradition
    records them, not because we recommend them.
  </p>
  <p>If you nevertheless choose to explore any practice, take these precautions:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Consult a physician first</strong> if you are pregnant or have a
      cardiovascular, respiratory, neurological, metabolic or psychiatric condition, or take
      medication that affects breathing, blood pressure or heart rate.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Never practise breath retention or forceful breathing alone or in water.</strong>
      Breath practices can cause dizziness, fainting, panic or, in susceptible people,
      serious harm.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Stop immediately</strong> if you experience chest pain, severe breathlessness,
      fainting, numbness, confusion, dissociation, or rising distress — and seek medical
      attention if symptoms persist.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not fast or restrict sleep</strong> if you are underweight, diabetic,
      pregnant, breastfeeding, taking medication that requires food, or under medical care.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Learn under qualified, accountable instruction</strong> where it is available,
      and prefer settings where someone can respond if you become unwell.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Treat your own stability as the priority.</strong> Research has documented that
      meditation can, in some practitioners, surface or intensify unpleasant experiences,
      including anxiety, depersonalization and traumatic memories. Intensity is not progress,
      and a practice that destabilises you is not a practice you should continue unassisted.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Intensive retreats deserve particular caution: they combine long sitting, disrupted
    sleep, isolation and, in some formats, fasting. If you have a history of psychosis,
    severe dissociation, bipolar disorder or trauma, discuss any retreat plan with a
    clinician who knows that history before attending.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Traditional and historical material</h2>
  <p>
    The site describes traditions as they are recorded — including claims made within those
    traditions — without asserting their truth, safety or present-day applicability. Legend
    is presented as legend, hagiography as hagiography, polemic as polemic. Where a claim
    comes from a tradition rather than from evidence, the site says so. Where modern
    research is cited, its study design, sample and limits are stated. The classification
    system used throughout is documented in the
    <a href="https://data.daoismhub.com/schemas/evidence-system/">Evidence System</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Translations and textual variants</h2>
  <p>
    Classical Chinese is compressed, allusive and often textually unstable. Translations on
    this site are original editorial renderings made for a general English-language reader;
    they are one documented reading among several, not the reading. Where the received text
    has variants, or where scholarship disputes an attribution, date or reading, the site
    flags it rather than resolving it silently. Anyone relying on a passage for scholarly
    work should consult the Chinese text and the standard critical editions and
    translations.
  </p>

  <h2>8. Accuracy, completeness and corrections</h2>
  <p>
    We work to keep content accurate, sourced and dated, and we correct errors when they are
    pointed out. We do not warrant that the sites are complete, current or free of error or
    omission. Content reflects the state of sources at the time of writing and is not
    continuously re-verified. Reference lists can contain links that later move, change or
    disappear. If you find a factual or translation error, please report it through the
    <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> — corrections are welcome and are part of how the
    site is maintained.
  </p>

  <h2>9. Practitioner experience records</h2>
  <p>
    The experience collection presents anonymized, paraphrased self-reports gathered from
    public community discussions. They are individual accounts. They are not evidence of
    benefit or safety, they are not representative of any population, they have not been
    clinically verified, and no conclusion about efficacy, risk or causality should be drawn
    from them. Where a report is unusual or contradicts other material, the site records the
    contradiction rather than smoothing it.
  </p>

  <h2>10. External links</h2>
  <p>
    Links to external sites — for example ctext.org for classical texts, or a journal for a
    cited study — are citations of record. We do not control those sites and are not
    responsible for their content, accuracy, availability or privacy practices. A link is
    not an endorsement, and a link that stops working is not a retraction of the citation.
  </p>

